{"alignments":[{"id":"alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7","layer":"US","nodeId":"math:quadratic-system-elimination","reason":"Elimination and real feasibility in quadratic systems: Algebraic elimination covers affine/quadratic systems and broader special quadratic systems; graphical solving is not an explicit criterion.","relation":"partial","reviewProvenance":{"effort":"medium","evidenceFile":"us-review-secondary.json","model":"gpt-6.1-sol","reviewMeaning":"Initial machine content review, not teacher review or an official crosswalk; no complete-standard mastery claim.","sourceReviewStatus":"partial"},"reviewStatus":"machine_content_review","sourceNodeId":"515","status":"accepted"}],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: -9/4","reason":"The intersection equation is x^2-3x-k=0. Its discriminant is 9+4k, which is zero exactly when k=-9/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1963_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_040","cnm_148","cnm_074"],"id":"solution:double-root-square:2bc807d40d99","label":"Complete the square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"double-root-square":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_221","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_221","cnm_220","cnm_040","cnm_148","cnm_074"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_040","cnm_219"],"id":"solution:double-root-discriminant:2bc807d40d99","label":"Use the discriminant","methodDirectNodeIds":{"double-root-discriminant":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_223"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_223","cnm_040","cnm_219"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, y = 4 only","reason":"The second equation gives x² = 3y - 12 ≥ 0, hence y ≥ 4. The first