b174c46017","label":"Split into zero and nonzero cases","methodDirectNodeIds":{"split-zero-product":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_220","cnm_h230"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_220","cnm_h230","cnm_040","cnm_081","cnm_151","cnm_075"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_040","cnm_081","cnm_151"],"id":"use-sum-and-difference","label":"Determine the sum and difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-sum-and-difference":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_148","cnm_224","cnm_090","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_148","cnm_224","cnm_090","cnm_h231","cnm_040","cnm_081","cnm_151"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 27 square units","reason":"Substituting y^2=25-x^2 into the second equation gives x^2+x-20=0. The intersections are (-5,0), (4,3), and (4,-3), forming a triangle of area 6×9/2=27.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12B_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_036","cnm_075","cnm_p148"],"id":"eliminate-factor-and-find-area","label":"Find the intersections, then the area","methodDirectNodeIds":{"eliminate-factor-and-find-area":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_084"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_084","cnm_148","cnm_036","cnm_075","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (a>1/2)","reason":"Substituting y=x^2-a into the circle equation gives x^2(x^2+1-2a)=0. The root x=0 always gives (0,-a). Exactly two additional distinct real roots, x=±sqrt(2a-1), occur precisely when a>1/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"eliminate-and-factor","label":"Eliminate and factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"eliminate-and-factor":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_220"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, y²+10y−7=0","reason":"Substituting x=−(y+3)/2 into the first equation and multiplying by 2 gives (y+3)²−6(y+3)+10y+2=0, which simplifies to y²+10y−7=0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1950_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_040","cnm_148","cnm_036"],"id":"eliminate-x","label":"Substitute from the linear equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"eliminate-x":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_040","cnm_148","cnm_036"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: a > 1/2","reason":"Substitution gives x^2 + (x^2 - a)^2 = a^2, or x^2(x^2 + 1 - 2a) = 0. The root x = 0 always gives one point. Exactly two additional points occur when x^2 = 2a - 1 > 0, so exactly three intersections occur precisely for a > 1/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_222","cnm_168"],"id":"eliminate-x-squared","label":"Solve the quadratic in y","methodDirectNodeIds":{"eliminate-x-squared":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_222","cnm_168","cnm_220","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, one point on the y-axis","reason":"At an intersection, ax²+bx+c=ax²-bx+c, so 2bx=0. Since b≠0, x=0 and y=c. Because c≠0, the unique point lies on the y-axis but is not the origin.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_042","cnm_082"],"id":"equate-the-two-graphs","label":"Cancel the shared terms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equate-the-two-graphs":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_083"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_083","cnm_042","cnm_082"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, two points","reason":"Substituting y²=9 into x²+y²=9 gives x²=0. The distinct common points are (0,3) and (0,-3).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_074"],"id":"eliminate-shared-square","label":"Eliminate the shared square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"eliminate-shared-square":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_220","cnm_082","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 136π","reason":"Tangency to the concentric circles of radii 2 and 8 forces (radius, center distance from the origin) to be (3,5) or (5,3). For either radius r, tangency to the third circle requires distance r+√3 or r-√3 from (5,0). Each distance gives two center locations, so there are four circles of each radius. Their total area is 4π·3²+4π·5²=136π.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"solve-center-distance-equations","label":"Verify centers with coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-center-distance-equations":["cnm_h181","cnm_h176","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_p192"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_h176","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_p192"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 136π","reason":"If d is the center's distance from the origin and r its radius, tangency to radii 2 and 8 forces (d,r) = (5,3) or (3,5). In each case, distance to (5,0) can be r + √3 or r - √3. Both distances lie strictly between |d - 5| and d + 5, so each gives two centers. There are four circles of each radius, totaling 4π·3² + 4π·5² = 136π.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"solve-center-distance-equations","label":"Verify centers with coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-center-distance-equations":["cnm_h181","cnm_h176","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_p192"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_h176","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_p192"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: √2","reason":"At an intersection, 10-x²=10-x, so x(x-1)=0. The points are (0,10) and (1,9), whose distance is √((1-0)²+(9-10)²)=√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_174"],"id":"find-intersections-and-distance","label":"Find the intersections and their distance","methodDirectNodeIds":{"find-intersections-and-distance":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_196","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_196","cnm_h173","cnm_082","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (2)","reason":"The radius is √r and the center-to-line distance is r/√2. Tangency gives r²/2=r; positivity excludes r=0 and leaves r=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_039","cnm_040"],"id":"one-intersection-discriminant","label":"Method 2: Require exactly one intersection","methodDirectNodeIds":{"one-intersection-discriminant":["cnm_260","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_223"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_260","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_223","cnm_250","cnm_039","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 2 miles per hour","reason":"Let v be the usual still-water speed and c the current. The time differences give v²-c²=6c and 4v²-c²=30c. Eliminating v² yields 3c²=6c, so c=2 because c>0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1966_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p111","cnm_156","cnm_147","cnm_161"],"id":"time-difference-equations","label":"Model the two time differences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"time-difference-equations":["cnm_166","cnm_165","math:quadratic-system-elimination"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_166","cnm_165","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_p111","cnm_156","cnm_147","cnm_161"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 5√2","reason":"Let A=(u,v) and B=(−u,−v). Adding their parabola equations gives 8u²−2=0, and subtracting gives v=7u. Thus the endpoints are ±(1/2,7/2), and AB=√(1²+7²)=5√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_036","cnm_220","cnm_174"],"id":"opposite-endpoint-coordinates","label":"Use the midpoint symmetry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"opposite-endpoint-coordinates":["cnm_249","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_249","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h173","cnm_082","cnm_036","cnm_220","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: (1, -2)","reason":"The root sum and product give a+b = -a and ab = b. Since b is nonzero, a = 1, then b = -2. Indeed, x²+x-2 = (x-1)(x+2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12B_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_029","cnm_040","cnm_034","cnm_151","cnm_146"],"id":"substitute-and-eliminate","label":"Substitute the solutions and check both cases","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-and-eliminate":["cnm_039","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_039","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_029","cnm_040","cnm_034","cnm_151","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 195/8","reason":"Let O be the circumcenter of ABC. Since OD⊥AB and OE⊥BC, the points B,D,E,O lie on the circle with diameter BO. Similarly C,E,F,O are concyclic, so X=O. Heron's formula gives area √(21·8·7·6)=84. Thus R=(13·14·15)/(4·84)=65/8, and XA+XB+XC=3R=195/8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_174","cnm_h177","cnm_224"],"id":"solution:coordinate-circle-equations:cdc75e126707","label":"Solve for the circle intersections","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-circle-equations":["cnm_265","cnm_h178","cnm_h173","math:quadratic-system-elimination"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_265","cnm_h178","cnm_h173","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_055","cnm_174","cnm_h177","cnm_224"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, a rectangle that is not a square","reason":"The four points are (3,4), (4,3), (-3,-4), and (-4,-3). Opposite points form diagonals of length 10 with common midpoint (0,0), so the quadrilateral is a rectangle. Its adjacent side lengths are √2 and 7√2, so it is not a square.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"],"id":"solution:equal-bisecting-diameters:4ce33274d5a3","label":"Method 1: Use the circle's diameters","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-bisecting-diameters":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h176","cnm_249","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"],"id":"solve-and-check-slopes","label":"Method 2: Check the side slopes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-and-check-slopes":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h167","cnm_h168","cnm_184","cnm_188"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_h167","cnm_h168","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_h173","cnm_191"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, filled upper parabolic cap","reason":"The coordinate edges map onto v=0,-1<=u<=1, and x=1,y=1 give u=1-v^2/4 and u=v^2/4-1. Necessity follows from (1-x^2)(1+y^2)>=0 and its swap. For sufficiency put R=sqrt(u^2+v^2), x=sqrt((R+u)/2), y=sqrt((R-u)/2). In the proposed cap v>=0 and v^2<=4(1-|u|), so R+|u|<=2; hence x,y are in [0,1] and map to (u,v). This proves the full filled image, not just its boundary, and matches actual choice D.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1969_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_029","cnm_096","cnm_074","cnm_148","cnm_147"],"id":"boundary-and-algebraic-preimage","label":"Map the sides and verify the entire region","methodDirectNodeIds":{"boundary-and-algebraic-preimage":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_220","cnm_231"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_220","cnm_231","cnm_082","cnm_029","cnm_096","cnm_074","cnm_148","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 8/9","reason":"The outer radius is 2 because its center lies on circle A of radius 1. Put the outer center at (0,0), A's center at (-1,0), and the other centers at (x,±r). Tangency gives x²+r²=(2-r)² and (x+1)²+r²=(1+r)². Hence x²=4-4r and x=3r-2, yielding r(9r-8)=0. Since r>0, r=8/9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_10A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_082","cnm_055","cnm_148"],"id":"center-distance-equations","label":"Use distances between centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-distance-equations":["cnm_h181","cnm_133","cnm_174","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_133","cnm_174","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_250","cnm_082","cnm_055","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 8/9","reason":"Let O be D's center and P be A's center. OP = 1, so D has radius 2. Set O = (0,0), P = (-1,0), and the equal circles' centers = (x,±r). Tangency gives x^2 + r^2 = (2-r)^2 and (x+1)^2 + r^2 = (1+r)^2. These imply x = 3r - 2 and r(9r - 8) = 0. Since r > 0, r = 8/9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_082","cnm_148","cnm_040"],"id":"solution:center-distance-equations:145925d87743","label":"Symmetry and distances between centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-distance-equations":["cnm_h181","cnm_133","cnm_174","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_133","cnm_174","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_042","cnm_250","cnm_082","cnm_148","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 784","reason":"The base side is 14, so the distances from E to lines AB and CD are 15 and 13. If its projection is distance y from AB and its height is h, then y^2+h^2=225 and (y-14)^2+h^2=169. Subtraction gives y=9, hence h=12. The volume is 196 times 12 divided by 3, or 784.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_065","cnm_148","cnm_042","cnm_075","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"],"id":"perpendicular-section","label":"Recover the height in a vertical section","methodDirectNodeIds":{"perpendicular-section":["cnm_h111","cnm_174","math:quadratic-system-elimination"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h111","cnm_174","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_p148","cnm_065","cnm_148","cnm_042","cnm_075","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":11,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c642af0b93792aea171aefe7"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 16/5","reason":"Set D = (0,0), A = (0,4), and M = (2,0). The circles give x² + y² = 4x and x² + y² = 8y, so the intersection other than D is P = (16/5,8/5). Its perpendicular foot lies on AD, giving distance 16/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_055","cnm_p188","cnm_064","cnm_082","cnm_148"],"id":"solution:circle-equations:27919bdc3556","label":"Coordinates and radius conditions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circle-equations":["cnm_084","cnm_174","cnm_h176","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_065"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_174","cnm_h176","math:quadratic-system-elimination","cnm_224","cnm_065","cnm_p077","cnm_055","cnm_p188","cnm_064","cnm_082","cnm_148"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["math:quadratic-system-elimination"],"label":"A-REI.C.7","layer":"US","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"code":"A-REI.C.7","description":"Solve a simple system consisting of a linear equation and a quadratic equation in two variables algebraically and graphically. For example,